    "lawCode": "Amd §§4900, 4905, 4910 & 4914, Pub Health L; amd §§4900, 4905, 4910 & 4914, Ins  L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A301\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law and the insurance\nlaw, in relation to protocol for treatment of rare disease\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:  Section 1 of the bill amends PHI. §4900\nto define rare disease treatment for disabling and life-threatening\nconditions and diseases, referencing federal guidelines.\n\nSections 2 and 4 amend PHL §§ 4905 and 4914 to establish evidentiary\nstandards for utilization review (UR) and external appeal when determin-\ning whether to pay for a rare disease treatment, using the standard\nalready in law for treatments under federally approved clinical trials:;\n\"likely to benefit\" the ,patient, providing that the treatment is not\nexcluded from coverage for other reasons.\n\nSection 3 amends PHI. §4910 to include the DR denial of payment for a\nrare disease treatment among the cases that can be taken to external\nappeal for review.\n\nSection 5-8 amends Insurance law §§ 4900,4905,4910 and 4914 to track the\nchanges made to PHL §§ 4900,4905,4910 and 4914.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: The utilization review and external appeal law works well\nto expand access to treatments in clinical trials by providing a special\nstandard for coverage determinations. However, the law fails to provide\nfor a small sub-class of individuals who are stricken with disabling and\nlife-threatening rare disease.\n\nThe ordinary standard for proving \"medical necessity\" does not apply for\npatients in clinical trials because, by definition, the treatment is\nstill on trial. So the utilization review and external appeal process\nuses the standard of \"likely to benefit.\"\n\nThis standard should apply to patients who need rare disease treatment.\nBecause there is little economic incentive for undertaking expensive\nclinical trails for rare disease treatments, these patients are also\ninherently unable to meet the ordinary standards for proving medical\nnecessity. A list of rare diseases is maintained by the National Insti-\ntutes of Health Office of Rare Diseases under the federal Rare Disease\nAct of 2002.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: A.8882-A: Third Reading Calendar\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately\n\n",
